// **This component is rendered by no page. Nothing imports this file.** That is the first
// fact about it, so it is the first thing written here rather than the last.
//
// - **`/nn/2026/` dropped it in commit 1b15cce**, on request. The "Know what you are in for"
//   section came off the page with the entry form on it, and this component was the whole of
//   that section's content.
// - **`/nn/` dropped it when the club supplied the course copy in full.** The four sections
//   now inside that page's card — where it goes, what it is like underfoot, what is on the
//   route, and the climb — restate every one of these five facts at greater length and in the
//   club's own wording. Keeping both would have printed the climb and the headphone rule
//   twice inside one card.
//
// ## The open question, which is a decision rather than a tidy-up
//
// This should either be **deleted** or **brought back into use**. Rendered-nowhere is a state
// nobody chose: it is what two separate copy decisions left behind, taken weeks apart and
// neither of them about this file. Meanwhile it goes on typechecking, goes on being formatted,
// and goes on reading as shared code to anybody who arrives from one of the notes that mention
// it. Deleting a component is cheap to undo from git; deciding that no page wants the short
// form of the course is not a call to make while editing markup. **Left for the humans, and
// written down here rather than taken.**
//
// **If it comes back, it comes back inside a `.nn-card`.** `.nn-arrows` is the second, latent
// instance of `NnSchedule`'s defect — `blood` markers and a `card-muted` divider, both computed
// against a white card surface — and it survived only because both call sites happened to sit
// in one. With no call sites left, nothing is holding that assumption up. See the note at
// `packages/shared/styles/nn-theme.css:1613` and the paragraph in CLAUDE.md before rendering
// this on any other surface.
//
// ---
//
// **Everything below is history.** It is why this markup reads the way it does, kept because
// the arguments cost something and because most of it is live again the moment any page renders
// these five lines. **None of it describes the site as it is today.**
//
// ## History — why there was one copy rather than two
//
// The five things somebody should know before they commit to this race, in one place because
// both pages that needed them needed exactly these five. `/nn/` showed them as *what this race
// is*, above the interest form; `/nn/2026/` showed them as *what you are about to enter*, above
// the entry form. Two copies of five safety-relevant bullets is two copies that drift, and the
// one that drifts is the one nobody re-reads — so there was one copy, here.
//
// The climb was pulled up from `/nn/course/` because it was reaching nobody who was about to
// enter: the page carrying the entry form said what the ground was like and what to put on your
// feet, and nothing about the shape of the thing being paid for. It sits second rather than
// last because the first bullet is what is underfoot and this is the shape that ground is in.
// "It is Bristol, in November. Plan for wet." came up with it, being the sentence that makes
// the trail-shoe line advice rather than trivia. **Both arrived word for word**, which is what
// let `site.spec.ts` assert the same sentences on three pages at once while `course.astro`
// existed; a tidier paraphrase here would have left three pages to drift instead of one
// component to maintain.
//
// The water station's location — approximately halfway, confirmed by the race director — was
// added because it is the fact somebody rations a bottle against, and this was then the only
// place it reached both pages. It is on `/nn/` now, in the club's own copy.
//
// ## History — the headphone rule, and why it is stated flatly
//
// **The rule itself is not history and is not decoration.** ARC's rules contain no headphone
// provision, but **Rule 81** lets an organiser make additional rules binding on competitors as
// though they were ARC's own — so this is the club's rule and it is enforceable, on a course
// Rule 83(3) shares with the public. It does not come off a page in a copy edit.
//
// **And it is stated flatly, with no "this one is absolute" after it.** That clause was on
// `course.astro` and came off before that page was absorbed into `/nn/` — not because the rule
// has softened, but because the fee structure carries a place for a visually impaired runner's
// guide and whether a guide is an exception to this has never been decided. "Absolute" settles
// that question in passing, in markup, which is not where it should be settled. Stating the
// rule and stopping leaves the committee the decision and leaves every runner reading it in no
// doubt about what is being asked of them.
//
// **This note was the course page's, inherited, and it has now been inherited once more.** The
// line it defends is on `/nn/` in the club's supplied copy, with the reasoning written beside
// it there. It stays here as well, because it travels with the markup rather than with the
// page: whoever brings this component back gets the rule and the reason for its wording in the
// same file.
//
// ## History — what was deliberately not in these five lines
//
// **A time.** The last bullet used to end "everybody walks down together at 10:30", and that is
// a row in one year's schedule rather than a property of the race. It belongs on
// `/nn/<year>/race-day/`, in the schedule the committee edits, and this said the part that is
// true whichever year it is run: you do not have to find the start on your own.
//
// **The walk is 10:40 as of 26 August 2026, which is the argument making itself.** Had the time
// stayed in the bullet it would by now be wrong wherever this rendered, and 10:30 would still
// have read as a real answer — it is the *briefing* under the revised schedule, so the stale
// number would have named a real thing happening ten minutes earlier, which is the kind of
// wrong that reads as right.
//
// **A distance from HQ to the start.** Nobody has supplied one, and deriving it from the
// coordinates and a guessed school entrance is exactly the plausible-looking number this
// repository keeps out of markup. See the note in `2026/race-day.astro`.
//
// **Where the route goes — and this one has since been overruled rather than forgotten.** It
// was kept out because `/nn/` already said it in the race director's own words, and a component
// that also said it would have put two spellings of one route on that page. The club has since
// asked for the full course copy on `/nn/`, which states the route a second time in the club's
// wording, and `site.spec.ts` asserts **both** spellings where it once refused the second. The
// objection was answered by a decision, not dropped.
//
// **The `{' '}` note in the markup below is in the present tense about two call sites that have
// gone.** It is left word for word because the trap belongs to this markup rather than to those
// pages, and it returns with the component: Prettier puts the `<strong>` on a line of its own,
// `compressHTML` removes the newline, and a typed space does not survive the reformat.
//
// Every line of copy here is the club's own 2023 wording and was **draft pending committee
// approval**, like the rest of these pages.
---

<ul class="nn-arrows">
  {
    /* **`{' '}` is a real space, and it is here because a line break is not.** Prettier keeps
    the `<strong>` on a line of its own, `compressHTML` removes the newline, and this rendered
    "slippery when wet.Trail shoes recommended" on both pages that use this component from the
    day it was extracted. The explicit space survives the reformat; a typed one does not. The
    same trap is documented at the head of `nn/privacy.astro` and in CLAUDE.md. */
  }
  <li>
    A tough, properly off-road course — uneven, rocky, rooty, and slippery when wet. It is
    Bristol, in November. Plan for wet.{' '}
    <strong>Trail shoes recommended</strong>, road shoes acceptable.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Nightingale Valley is the climb.</strong> It is the part people remember, and the
    part that decides how the rest of your race feels. There is no clever way to run it — go
    up steadily, and enjoy how much better everything looks from the top.
  </li>
  <li><strong>No headphones of any type during the race.</strong></li>
  <li>
    One water station on the route at approximately halfway, one at the finish, and
    marshals throughout.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The start and the finish are not at race HQ.</strong> Everybody walks down together
    beforehand, so there is nothing to find on your own.
  </li>
</ul>
